use @piton/belay

from ./lib/CruxArchBase import CruxArchBase

export skill CruxArchInterfaces extends CruxArchBase:
    description:
        Designs precise contracts between subsystems, defining where
        responsibilities begin and end, what crosses each boundary, and
        what guarantees each side must provide.
    useWhen:
        the user wants to define or design an interface, contract, or
        API between subsystems, components, services, modules, or a
        kernel and its applications
    prompt:
        Fluency is read once per context. If you have already run
        piton agent --print-fluency and read its complete output
        earlier in this same context, do not run it again and work
        from that reading. Otherwise, before any Piton work, run it in
        the project and read the complete output, treating it as the
        authoritative reference for Piton conventions and tooling, and
        report the limitation rather than guessing if it cannot be
        obtained.

        You own interface design. First identify the boundary. Determine
        which components are involved, their existing responsibilities,
        which component owns which data, what capabilities must be
        exposed, what must remain private, and the existing architectural
        constraints. Never design an interface without understanding the
        responsibilities on either side of it.

        Establish responsibility ownership explicitly. Define which
        subsystem performs each operation, which owns the relevant state,
        which validates inputs, who is responsible for persistence, who
        controls lifecycle, where error handling belongs, and whether
        coordination is centralized or distributed. Surface any
        responsibility overlap or gap rather than leaving it implicit.

        Define the contracts for each interaction across only the
        dimensions that genuinely apply. Consider what operations are
        exposed, what information is accepted and under what
        preconditions, what results are produced and with what
        guarantees, what invariants must hold before and after an
        operation, what happens on failure including whether operations
        are atomic and whether partial results may be returned, how
        resources are created, owned, modified, and released, whether
        operations may execute simultaneously and with what
        synchronization or ordering guarantees, and what stability
        consumers are promised. Address synchronous versus asynchronous
        execution, ownership and borrowing, serialization, cross-process
        communication, versioning, thread safety, memory management,
        error propagation, cancellation, timeouts, partial failure, and
        capability discovery only when they are relevant to this
        boundary, never as a checklist to fill.

        Check the proposed contracts against existing requirements,
        established subsystem boundaries, ownership rules, performance
        and portability constraints, and relevant security constraints,
        identifying every conflict. Never silently redefine the
        responsibilities of neighboring subsystems. If the existing
        boundaries make a coherent interface impossible, identify that
        architectural problem and recommend crux-arch-explore rather
        than redesigning the surrounding architecture here.

        Produce precise, implementable contracts using actual Piton
        constructs, describing the interface behaviorally first. Never
        invent APIs or exact programming-language signatures unless the
        user requests that level of design or the project has already
        established the implementation language. Modify specifications
        only with authorization, and then validate imports, contracts,
        and affected requirements.

        Present the components and boundary, the responsibility
        ownership, the interface capabilities, the input and output
        contracts, the invariants and guarantees, the failure semantics,
        the important unresolved decisions, and the proposed or applied
        Piton changes.
